# Design docs

## Locations

| Document | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `docs/design.ja.md` | Canonical design (Japanese). Contracts, invariants, phases |
| `docs/design-decisions.md` | Rejected alternatives and why (Decision / Reason / Rejected) |
| `docs/adr/NNNN-*.md` | One decision per file whose reason is a measurement (Status, Date, Issue, Context, Decision) |
| `docs/roadmap.md` | Rows: ID, title, Issue, status. No acceptance text |
| `docs/references.md` | Which upstream file to read before a layer is built |
| `docs/conventions.md` | Directory tree, project responsibilities, coding conventions |

`.dev/` is local scratch and is gitignored. Do not put lasting decisions there.

## Changing the design

- Contract, public API, invariant, or phase: open a `design` Issue, update `docs/design.ja.md` (and `docs/design-decisions.md` for what was rejected) in its PR, and merge it before implementation Issues.
- Wording, typos, and examples: may ship in the same PR as code.
- Do not implement first and update the design later. If code and design conflict, stop and fix the design through the process above.
- An implementation that takes a narrower form of a design sentence (a rule applied to some pointers only) writes it into the design and into the PR's decisions before the PR is called ready, with the OS document when the OS's behavior is the reason. I1a called `IPointerPlatform.Capture` for the mouse alone, since Win32 captures a touch and a pen in contact by itself (Microsoft's `WM_POINTERDOWN` page), while §7 "Capture" still said to call it; the review found it (PR #389). A wider form, a rule applied to a case the design does not name, is written the same way. I1b's `HoldScroll` kept every press on a box being dragged out of the box, the mouse's included, while §7 said so only of a box that flings; the review found it too (PR #397). Before the PR is called ready, take each branch on the path the PR changes and find the design sentence that states it. A call into an outside component (an OS API, a COM object) is checked the same way for when and how often it is made: I1c called DirectManipulation's `Update` in every frame of every window that had a viewport, while §7 polled it only from an interaction's begin to its end; it was the likely cause of the Desktop tests that stopped twice on the CI's Windows runner, and PR #420 fixed it.
- A row of a table of keys says what the key does with each modifier the platform's controls give a meaning, Shift above all, and says it when the key is left to go on. P02b's table named Enter alone; in a field of many lines, Shift+Enter neither broke the line nor was stopped, while Avalonia and zed break the line with it, and the decision was made after the review (PR #489).
- An implementation that changes when or how many of an object are made (an allocation, a cache, a table) searches the design for the sentences about that object and corrects them in the same PR. P02b2's area made a leaf's selection at its first press for every leaf it holds, while §3.2 still said only a selected leaf made one; the review found it (PR #503).
- Before a design PR is opened, each section that its Issue's "Sections to change" names is opened and checked: changed, or the Issue says why not. P02b2's Issue named §7 and §2.14, the PR changed neither, and the review found that the cursor's walk (§7) and the frame clock's list (§2.14) did not say what §4.10 needed (PR #496).
- A design PR that gathers a decision in a new section adds a sentence that points to it in each other section the Issue's "Sections to change" lists. D9's design put rich text in a new §4.8 and left §2.13, §4.1, §5.0, and §7 as they were, so a reader of one of them alone would miss what changes there; the review asked for the four sentences (PR #418). Each section that the list names is changed before the PR, or the Issue says why it is not: P02a's Issue listed the place of the test ID in §2.6 or §7, and the API listing of §2.6 was left without `TestId` until the review (PR #457).

A comparison of approaches in the design includes the approach of each framework that a measurement found fastest, judged by the requirements. In L4 Flutter and Avalonia were the fastest, while §3.1 compared neither Flutter's box constraints nor Avalonia's measure and arrange beyond a reason of structure; the owner's question reopened it (D4, PR #105).

A cited line range holds every item the sentence names from it. PR #515 cited WPF's `CompositionAdorner.cs` as lines 159 to 222 for solid, dotted, dashed, and wavy lines; the wavy case is line 225, outside the range, and the review found it.

A claim in the design about what another implementation has or does (a library version, a framework's widget, a browser) names the file and line, or the documentation page, where it was read, and is read there before it goes in. In L3 the Grill wrote that Taffy 0.15 has sizing keywords in min and max; its `min_size` and `max_size` are `LengthPercentageAuto` (`style/mod.rs` lines 757 and 760), which the implementation found (PR #134). It was the third such error after Flutter's `Offstage` and Taffy's containing block in L2. The claim keeps the conditions the source states. V1b's design wrote that GPUI neither draws nor presents a frame that is not dirty; the source presents without drawing while a drawn frame has not been presented or input comes at a high rate (`needs_present`), which the subagent's report had stated and the design left out (PR #309's review). A file and line that a subagent's report gives is read at the cited version by the agent that writes the design, before it goes in. P02d's design took four lines from the reports as they were, and the review found all four off: one line early (`editable_text.dart` 5208 for 5201), a blank line (`raw_menu_anchor.dart` 745), another method's declaration (`context_menu.rs` 739 to 750), and a call to another function (`mouse.rs` 345, `prepaint_as_root` and not `layout_as_root`) (PR #548).

A mechanism the design takes from a compared implementation is written with the arguments and flags that the cited lines pass to keep it correct. P02b's design took GPUI's way of telling AltGr apart, a second `ToUnicode` with Ctrl, Alt, and Win removed, and cited the lines that pass `0x4` ("do not change the keyboard state") to both calls; the design did not say so, and without the flag the second call breaks a dead key's composition. The review found it (PR #482).

A comment in the code that says what another implementation does is held to the same: the file and line are read before the comment is written. AN1's `NodeTransitions` said that Avalonia's `BrushTransition` changes a gradient halfway; Avalonia 12.1.4 mixes gradients (`BrushTransition.cs` lines 26 to 41), which came out only when the design needed the citation (PR #476, row AN2).

A rule or a list taken from a specification (a UAX, CSS) is copied from the specification's section, read for the purpose, and the section is named. T1's design wrote the mandatory line breaks from memory as five kinds, and left out UAX #14's BK characters VT, FF, and U+2029 (LB4, LB5); the same design required the conformance tests to pass with no exception, so an implementation that followed it would have failed them. The review found it (PR #281). Read the specification's text itself: download its HTML and read the section, not a summarizing tool's answer. In T1a WebFetch summarized UAX #14 and would not return the rules in full; the downloaded page showed that Unicode 18.0.0 had changed GB9c and LB12a (PR #285).

A design does not say what all the compared frameworks do or lack when one of them was not read: read it (fetch the file at the same commit) or leave it out of the claim. T1b's design said that none of the three reads HarfBuzz's unsafe-to-break flag while its PR said `SkShaper` had not been read; Skia's `SkShaper_harfbuzz.cpp` reads it (lines 970 and 1563), which the review found (PR #289).

A decision that changes one case of an algorithm (the width of a soft hyphen in the fit test) is written together with the algorithm's general rule (take the last opportunity that fits). T1b's first sentence read as "if it does not fit, break in an emergency", against the rule that falls back to an earlier opportunity (PR #289).

A sentence that says a list is the same as another implementation is checked item by item in that implementation's source, and an item it lacks is written as XylemUI's own. The T2 design said its three conditions for ClearType were GPUI's; GPUI has two, and the third (the pixel geometry is not `FLAT`) was XylemUI's own addition. The review found it (PR #179).

A design that connects to an OS protocol writes both of its directions: the calls the OS makes into the app, and the calls the app makes to tell the OS (a sink, a notification), with when each is sent. P02c's first design wrote `ITextInputClient`, what TSF calls to read and change a field, and left out `ITextStoreACPSink`'s `OnTextChange`, `OnSelectionChange`, and `OnLayoutChange`, which TSF needs when the app changes the field itself; the review found it (PR #507).

How a value the OS hands over is drawn (an IME's display attributes) is decided after reading the implementations that read that value, not only the compared frameworks that ignore it. P02c's Grill looked at Flutter, Avalonia, and GPUI, which draw one underline, and chose a thin solid line for the other clauses; spike #510 found that the IME (Google Japanese Input) asks for a dotted line, and Chromium and WPF draw the line style and the thickness the IME asks for (PR #511).

A rule that acts between neighbours (a gap between an IME's clause underlines) says what it does at the two ends of the run as well. PR #519 wrote that each clause's underline is shortened at both ends, and left open whether the composition's first and last ends are; the review found it.

A sentence that names what the field tells the OS after a change names everything the change moves. PR #519 wrote that a commit cut to the maximum length sends `TextChanged`; the cut moves the caret too, so `SelectionChanged` goes with it, which the review found.

A design that uses a value read from the OS says which unit it is read for (the system, or the window's monitor), when it is read, and when it is read again. The T2 design said only that the ClearType setting is read at startup, and left open which monitor the pixel geometry comes from and whether a change of setting or of monitor reads it again (PR #179 review, Q9).

How a cross-platform library draws on one OS is read on that OS's path: the font manager, scaler, or backend it uses there. In the T4 Grill the agent wrote that Skia rasterizes with FreeType on Windows; Flutter and SkiaSharp use Skia's DirectWrite font manager there (`txt/src/txt/platform_windows.cc`), and its scaler does not reach FreeType (`SkScalerContext_win_dw.cpp`). The rasterizer had to be asked again (PR #158).

When the version of a compared framework changes, the claims that cite the old version are checked again in the new one. In L9 the design cited "the Taffy 0.9.0 that GPUI uses" seven times; Zed ships 0.13.0, where two of the claims (no sizing keywords, no `safe` alignment) no longer hold (PR #140).

A design PR that changes a decision searches the whole design for the sections that restate it, and changes them in the same PR. In F1 the PR changed §4.5's bundled font, and §8.12, §4.6, §11's test mode, §4.1's diagram, and the table of failures still assumed the old one; the review found the five (PR #146).

A design that changes the shape of a type rewrites every example in the design that uses the type so that it works with the new shape; changing the comment beside it is not enough. V1a's design made `UiNode.Text` a nullable `TextLeaf`, and §2.4's `Setter` still called `n.Text.SetContent(v)`, which the new type did not have, with only its comment changed; the review found it (PR #297). The same holds when a member's shape changes, and before the PR the design is searched for each changed name: P02a's design made `Find(string)` return `Element`, and §12.2 still read `Find<TextNode>("clicks").Text` and §12.5 `FindAll(role: ...)` (PR #457). The search covers the words of the decision, not only the names it adds: P02a2's design moved the readback and did not search for `ReadPixel` and "readback", and four restatements stayed (the roadmap row, §12.1, §12's list of rows, and §12's "Core との関係", PR #465). A design that replaces one technology with another searches for the old technology's name as well: P02c's design moved from IMM32 to TSF, searched only the old type names, and left IMM32 in §2.9.1's folder table (PR #507). A sentence added to a paragraph that states an older decision is read with the rest of that paragraph: D7 added to §8.1 that the `exit` argument became `motion` and left the paragraph's last sentence saying `exit` would be added (PR #472).

Before a row's design is written, the whole design is searched for the row's ID, and every contract an earlier row sent to it is taken into the new section. T2 had left "glyphs inside opacity, transforms, and layers are grayscale; the implementation is in D10 and V1", and D10's first design brought all three without it; the review found it (PR #239).

A mechanism applied per command (a clip, a brush) says which kinds of command it applies to, glyph runs included. D10's first design left open whether the rounded clip applies to text (PR #239). One applied to text says what it does for each kind of glyph: grayscale, ClearType, color, and color with a layer in the text's color. R4b's first design gave color glyphs the brush's alpha and left open which text color keys a layer drawn in the text's color in the atlas (PR #252).

A type that takes a visual value (a blur radius, how a gradient interpolates, whether a shadow shows under its shape) says what each value means, after listing how each compared implementation reads it, with lines. R4's first design said none of the three; the four implementations read a blur radius four ways (CSS σ = r / 2, Flutter σ = 0.57735 r + 0.5, Avalonia σ = 0.288675 r + 0.5, GPUI σ = r), and the review's own figure for Flutter was wrong when read in the source (PR #243).

An option a Grill offers relies only on what the code or the design already has (a font metric, a type, a value from the OS), checked before the Grill; an option that needs something new says so and offers adding it. R4b's Q11 made the default box of brushed text from the font's ascender and descender, which `Font` does not have; writing the design found it, and the Grill was reopened (PR #252). It holds for where a measurement runs: V3c's Q6 measured each operation's frame stages in the sample through an `--auto` mode, while the sample uses only the public API, which has no stage times; the implementation found it, and the owner chose a benchmark scene that references the sample instead (PR #381). D9's Grill broke it three times: Q9 skipped the underline at glyphs, which needs glyph outlines the rasterizer does not give; Q2 took wavy lines and shadows, which need curves and a blur the display list does not have; Q3 made a node for each link range, which the v1 accessibility model of one node per `UiNode` cannot hold. Writing the design found all three, and the owner chose again as Q12 to Q15 (PR #418). A recommendation that covers two kinds of thing at once (menus and modal dialogs) is checked for each kind before the round is sent, and the type of a value it offers is checked against who writes the value. P02d's Q4 closed both kinds when the window is deactivated, which would close a dialog on Alt+Tab, and Q9 took a read-only signal for the open state, which the popup writes when it closes itself; writing the design changed both (PR #548). A default that depends on another option is written for each case: `PopupOptions.DismissOnOutsidePress` defaulted to closing, and a modal popup would then close on a press on its backdrop, against §7.3; the review found it (PR #548).

A claim about another implementation that a Grill question makes (what it does, as a reason for an option) is read in its source before the round is sent, as a claim in the design is. V1a's Grill said that Flutter splits the extra space of a line-height multiplier half above and half below, and that its `paint` draws the one result its layout made; in the source the default scales ascent and descent by the same ratio (`Run.cpp`), and `paint` lays out again (`paragraph.dart`). Writing the design found both (PR #297). It happened again in V1d: Q21 gave Avalonia's default `FontSize` as 14, and writing the design found 12 (`TextElement.cs` line 40); the owner chose again from the sources (PR #336). A behavior read for a question is read to its end, not only its start: I1b's Q8 said that Flutter drags with the latest finger, and writing the design found what happens when that finger lifts (the first remaining finger, `monodrag.dart` lines 781 to 782), which then had to be decided outside the Grill (PR #393).

A recommendation that picks a value other than a compared implementation's puts that value and the reason for the difference in the question itself. I1b's Q2 recommended a slop of 18 for a container that scrolls on both axes; Flutter's two-axis drag uses `kPanSlop`, 36, which neither the question nor the design said, and the review found it (PR #393).

The rejected options a later question adds to `docs/design-decisions.md` go after the last one of the question before, so that each question's read together. PR #408 put Q12's and Q13's between two of Q11's, and the review asked to move them.

Before a Grill offers an option, search `docs/design-decisions.md` and the design's records of rejected options for it. The same holds for a sentence of the design that says how a layer implements something (how to write its COM, which library to call): search the layer's rule and the rejected options first. P02c's design wrote that TSF's COM is source-generated (`GeneratedComInterface`), while `interop.mdc` writes the COM the OS calls back as hand-written vtables and `docs/design-decisions.md` had rejected source-generated COM for its size; writing the implementation's Issue found it (Issue #535). An option rejected before is named as such in the question, with the old reason and why it no longer holds. I1c's Q10 offered telling the scheduler whether a frame presented, which S7 had rejected as needing a new link between the renderer and the platform; the question did not say so, and writing the design found it (PR #401).

A checkout under `.repos/` can hold part of a repository: `.repos/flutter` lacked `gestures/converter.dart`, `binding.dart`, `arena.dart`, `widgets/scroll_position.dart`, and the Windows embedder's headers, and `.repos/avalonia12` lacks `Avalonia.Controls` and the macOS backend. A file that is not there is not evidence that the implementation lacks it. Fetch the file at the same tag (`raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/<repo>/<tag>/<path>`), read it, and delete the copy (PR #401).

Before a line of a compared implementation is cited, the version of its checkout under `.repos/` is read with `git describe --tags` and compared with the version the design names. Before I1b's Grill, `.repos/flutter` was at 3.47.7 and `.repos/zed` at v1.24.3, while the design cites Flutter 3.47.8 and zed v1.25.3; lines read from another version can move without notice (PR #393).

A design that allocates by a size the content decides (an offscreen layer as large as its children's bounds) writes what cuts the size (the clip, the target), the cap, and what is drawn when the cap refuses it. R4c's first design said a layer is as large as its bounds; a group around scrolling content or a far transform could ask for a texture past D3D11's 16,384 pixels a side or past 1 GB, and the review found it (PR #260).

A design that adds a new kind of edge (a clip) says whether it is put on pixels by the rounding the other edges take (W1), and under the same condition. Without it, a child's rounded edge and the clip's unrounded edge are half a pixel apart, and a thin line or a gap shows at the clip's border (PR #260).

A new public command states what it does with an argument that is NaN or out of range, as the commands before it do (a `Stroke`'s width throws `ArgumentOutOfRangeException`). R4c's first design left `PushOpacity`'s alpha open (PR #260).

Every type in a signature the design writes is defined in the design or the code. F1's first `IFontProvider` took `in FontStyle style`, which nothing defined, so whether it carried the language could not be read (PR #146).

A design that changes the unit of a quantity (logical against physical pixels) lists every API that carries it across a boundary, arguments and return values, and writes the unit of each. W1's first design gave the units of the display list's coordinates and of the window's size, and left out the size handed to the layout root (`NotifyWindowResized`) and the advance `DrawText` returns; the review found both (PR #232). Input coordinates are such an API too, and so is every section that converts them: R4e's design took hit regions in logical pixels, kept entries in physical pixels, and did not say which unit `HitTest` receives, while §6.1.1 divides pointer coordinates by the scale (PR #273).

A design that leaves a point to another row ("row X decides") names a row that exists in `docs/roadmap.md`, and that row's text names the point, in the same PR (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`). R4e's design left one point to "the input row", which does not exist, and another to row V1, whose text did not mention it; the review found both (PR #273).

A design that reads values from an OS message names, for each value, the field of the message it comes from, or the call that reads it when the message has none, after reading the message's documentation. V1c's first design read Control, Shift, and Alt from the mouse messages together; the `wParam` of `WM_MOUSEMOVE` has `MK_CONTROL` and `MK_SHIFT` and no Alt, which `GetKeyState(VK_MENU)` reads (PR #319's review). A value it takes from an OS structure is written with the range and the direction the OS documentation gives, read there; a value left without them because the sources at hand did not say is read in the documentation instead. I1's design wrote the pen's tilt "as `POINTER_PEN_INFO` has it", while that page gives −90 to +90 degrees, positive to the right and toward the user (PR #385's review). The order in which the OS sends its messages is read before the design decides where a state ends: I1's first design returned a touch's number at its Up, and Windows sends a `WM_POINTERLEAVE` after the Up of a touch too (PR #385's review).

A design that names the type or method a call goes in reads which project that type is in and what the project may reference. W1's design put Win32's `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext` in `UiThread.Initialize`, which is in Core, where no OS type may be referenced; the implementation found it, and the owner moved the call to the first window (PR #236).

A type in a design signature that holds a size is checked against the scale factors of §6.1 (125 %, 150 %). T4's first `GlyphKey` took `int PixelSize`, which cannot hold the 17.5 px of a 14 px font at 125 % (PR #158 review).

A design that says an OS API does something names the version that has that capability (the interface number and the overload), after reading its declaration in the SDK the build uses. The T6 design took `IDWriteFactory4::TranslateColorGlyphRun` from GPUI's code for COLRv1 too; the paint-tree runs come only from the overload of `IDWriteFactory8` that takes a `DWRITE_PAINT_FEATURE_LEVEL` (Windows SDK 10.0.26100), and the review found it (PR #188).

A design whose text names a member of a type (V2's "the amount goes to Core as `PointerInput.WheelDelta`") adds that member to the type in the same section's code block. V2's design added `WheelDelta` to `PointerEvent` and not to `PointerInput`, and the review found it (PR #344).

A design that fixes the shape of a public C# API compiles its signatures and its sample code in a scratch project with the repository's settings (implicit usings, C# 14, `InvariantGlobalization`) before the design PR writes them. V1d's design compiled none of it, and the implementation met five diagnostics in its shape: `TextWriter` against the implicit `System.IO.TextWriter` (CS0104), an interpolated-string handler that cannot take a member's receiver by `ref` (CS1620), a `ref` field to a `ref struct` (CS9050), `Text(...)` hidden by the namespace `XylemUI.Text` inside `XylemUI.*` (CS0118), and a public event that showed the internal `NodeHandle` (CS0053); and `CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture`, which `InvariantGlobalization` makes invariant whatever the OS's language is (PR #340).

A design that draws with an OS component or a format it has not used measures the first-use cost and the memory in a scratch prototype before the Grill decides. The T6 Grill chose Direct2D for every color format, COLRv1 included; the implementation measured 4 ms for a first emoji and +8.5 MB, and Q11 went back to COLRv0 (PR #192). The prototype draws at the scales of §6.1 (100, 125, and 150 %); T8's first one drew at 125 % only. A scene can take the scale from an argument in the scratch build, which leaves the display's scale alone (PR #200).

A design that picks the side a compared implementation avoids for a stated reason writes what that reason costs, and its acceptance measures it. Chromium rounds advances for bilevel glyphs to keep their spacing even; T8 kept fractional advances and did not say that the spacing then varies by 1 px, and the review found it (PR #200).

A design that says it does what another implementation does names the parameters that are the same. T8 wrote "as Chromium and Firefox" for bilevel glyphs; only `ALIASED` and `ALIASED_1x1` were the same, and both pair them with the `GDI_CLASSIC` measuring mode (PR #200's review).

What a compared implementation does is checked in its shipped build when its source puts the code behind a build condition. Skia has a COLRv1 path under `#if` for the Windows 11 SDK; Flutter and Avalonia drew COLRv0 on screen (PR #192). An OS condition is checked the same way: Impeller draws light text white only on macOS (`ComputeTone` in `font_glyph_pair.h`), so on Windows every glyph takes Skia's black-text table, and a model with the white-text table moved away from Flutter on black (PR #196).

A model that reproduces a compared implementation's pixels reads every stage a pixel passes through, from the rasterizer to the fragment shader. In T7 Skia's preblend alone matched Flutter on white but not on black; Impeller's `glyph_atlas.frag` also raises the coverage to an exponent from the text luminance (`text_contents.cc` lines 282 to 295), found later (PR #196).

A design that reads data from a font or a file treats it as untrusted input and writes its limits (size, how much it inflates or allocates, the structure it accepts) and what happens past them. T9's first design said its PNG decoder read every form of the format, with no limits; the review found it (PR #208).

Those limits bound what is allocated, not only what is read: a size the input claims is checked against what the input can hold (the compressed data's length times the format's largest inflation) before a buffer of that size is made, and a test measures the allocation of the worst input. T9's decoder stopped inflating at the size the header asked for, but allocated that size first, so a 68-byte PNG made it allocate 128 MB; the review found it (PR #212).

A design that calls an OS API from several threads where its documentation says nothing about threads checks correctness apart from speed: the parallel result is the serial one, byte for byte, over hundreds of repetitions. T5's first design measured only time and allocation; the review found it (PR #219).

A design that adds threads or OS resources writes their lifetime: who creates them, when they stop, who releases them, and what happens when they cannot be created. T5's first design said resident threads and a rasterizer for each, and no more; the review found it (PR #219).

A design split into two steps (room left now, filled later) says which arrays the later step rewrites (compacts, splits) and which version their readers see. The review of T5 found that the first design did not (PR #219). A design that adds a value to a command such a step rewrites says how the step carries it: R4b's first design said how a gradient is drawn on glyphs, and not that `ResolveGlyphs`, when its `Rebuild` splits a run at a color glyph, copies the run's brush to both halves; the review found it (PR #252).

A design that puts outside data into the repository (test images, fonts) names its source, its version, and its license, quoted from the file in the distribution. T9's first design named PngSuite without them; the review found it (PR #208).

A figure the design derives (a memory bound, a count) writes its arithmetic beside it. From T2 the design said both atlas kinds held 4 MB; a ClearType kind holds 16 pages of 1 MB, 16 MB, and an answer to a review found it (PR #192).

A design that keeps a cache or a state per node writes its size per node as a formula of the content (characters, glyphs, lines, entries), and its acceptance bounds it with a number a test checks. Reporting the size is not a bound. V1a's first design only reported the size of a text leaf; with T1b's initial arrays a leaf could hold 5 to 12 KB, 50 to 120 MB for 10,101 leaves, and the review found it (PR #297).

A design that keeps a state in an array indexed by the node's slot says what that state is when the slot is used again for a new node. V3b's design said that a new node starts with its remembered overflow marked stale, and not that a freed node's slot keeps the old node's value and mark until the new node resets them in `CreateNode`; the review found it (PR #373).

A value that only some nodes use goes behind a reference of its own, made for the nodes that use it. Fields added to a side object that many nodes hold are paid by every node that holds it. V1c's first design put the hovered and pressed `PaintStyle?` (about 200 bytes) in `NodeVisual`, which every node with a background holds: about 2 MB for 10,000 rows of a list. The review found it, and the owner moved them to `PseudoStyles` (PR #319).

A contract that hands work to an OS API is written after reading that API's output parameters, not only its inputs: what it returns when it finds nothing, and what else it returns. F1's first interface dropped `MapCharacters`'s `scale` and left the length of a range that no font renders undefined; the documentation defines both (PR #146).

A public API that reads OS state when it is asked (a pointer's coalesced points) says which layer reads it, where the result is kept, and until when it can be read, the time the OS keeps the state included. I1's design said only that `GetIntermediatePoints()` builds the points from the history when it is read; how Core's `PointerEvent` reaches the Platform's history, and that Win32 keeps the history of the thread's last pointer message only, were left out, and the review asked for both (PR #385).

A design that removes a dependency lists every place that calls it now and what replaces each. T4 removed FreeType from Windows without saying that `Font` creates a FreeType face when it opens and reads `FamilyName` from it (PR #158 review).

A design that changes what a layout or render algorithm reads checks every cache and boundary rule against the change, the path that hits a cache included. In L1 a review found that "a child whose size the parent decided is a boundary" does not hold with the automatic minimum size, wrapping, and baseline alignment, and the re-review found that a mark set again in each run was lost in a run that hit the cache (PR #75, Issue #76).

A design of what drives a frame or a loop lists every call that the code driving it today makes (a sample, a benchmark scene) and checks each against its own steps. V1b's first design left out `GlyphAtlas.BeginFrame`, which `benchmarks/XylemUI.E2E/Scene.cs` calls before every display list; without it no place of the atlas can be evicted (PR #309's review). A design that leaves one of those calls out of some frames says what the call guards and how those frames make up for it. P02d's design skipped `BeginFrame` in a frame that builds a popup's list alone, and said that the skip keeps the window's glyphs from being evicted. It did not say that an atlas at its page limit then refuses the popup's new glyphs, and draws none of them until the next `BeginFrame`. The review found it, and such a frame now has the next one build the window's list (PR #552).

A design that stops calling a node's handlers (a disabled node) lists the states those handlers would have cleared, and clears them where it stops the calls. K1b's button cleared its Space press in its focus-out handler; a button disabled while Space was down lost the focus with that handler skipped, and stayed pressed once enabled again. The review found it, and `UiTree.SetDisabledStates` now clears the pressed state of the nodes it disables (PR #360).

A design in which a part does something through handlers it adds (a button's keys) names the API each handler calls, and checks that the part's project may call it. K1's design called the button's Enter and Space "ordinary handlers"; routing a click from a key and setting the pressed state had no public API, and the implementation asked the owner to let `XylemUI.Primitives` see Core's internals (PR #360).

A design that calls a user's handler in the middle of changing its own state says what happens when the handler comes back into it: a frame run synchronously, a message pumped inside the handler. V1c's design did not; the review found that a rehit from a leave handler rewrote the hover chains being sent (PR #327), and the re-review found the same through the window's leave (Issue #329).

A mechanism where a stage clears marks only for the items of a list (the paint updates) says that every path that sets the mark puts the item in the list. V1b's design said a frame clears the marks of its paint updates, and the layout changes set the paint mark without the list; the mark stayed, and a later change asked for no frame (Issue #324).

A state that only the draw stage moves on says how it ends when the draw skips the node (the node or an ancestor hidden). AN1's transitions moved their values only while the node was drawn; a node hidden mid-transition kept the window asking for frames that changed nothing, which the review found (PR #476).

A decision that changes what happens under a condition (a frame that did not present) is written for every path on which the condition holds, read against the mechanism, not only for the path that led to it. I1c's first §2.11 had the scheduler wait on the timer when the next frame was asked for inside a callback that did not present; the swap chain's object stays unsignaled after such a callback whoever asks next, so a frame asked for later by a hover change still waited 50 ms. The review found it (PR #401).

A state that one event starts (the wheel dropped, frames polled) ends on an event that comes on every path from that start. I1c's design dropped the wheel from `SetContact` until PanEnd, and polled DirectManipulation from `SetContact` until READY; two fingers that rest and lift without moving never make the viewport run, so neither end came, and the window would have dropped the mouse's wheel and asked for frames from then on. Writing the implementation found the second (PR #408's Q12) and the review the first (PR #406). Follow each path from the start, the ones where nothing happens included, to the event that ends the state.

A design in which pending work asks for the next turn of a loop (a frame, a flush) says which work asks for it, including work a turn puts back after a failure. V1b cleared the frame request at the frame's start, so the request the layout pass put back after a measurement threw asked for a new frame each time, and a leaf that kept throwing drew and presented every frame (PR #313's review).

A value a later stage needs that the layout resolves by rules that differ case by case (a percentage of a border, resolved against the parent's width, the outer width, a flex container's inner width, or a containing block) is kept from the layout's commit and read, not resolved again. V1b's design first had the draw stage resolve the border widths against the parent's content box, which differs from the layout for an absolutely positioned child and a parent of undecided width (PR #309's re-review).

An acceptance that a change allocates nothing is checked against each API that makes the change. V1b's first design said a change of color allocates nothing, while the only way to change a box's color was to hand the tree a new `NodeVisual` object (PR #309's review).

A design that stops a propagation with a comparison decides two things in the design itself: everything the propagation carried, and what happens when a value to compare is gone. In L5 both came from reviews: the measurement cache (4 entries) can overwrite a value the parent read, and the path's `SubtreeDirty` carried Paint, HitTest, and Text with Layout, so stopping at the parent left the repaint with no carrier (PR #109).

A design that relies on an invariant says where the state it rests on may change and where it must not, in a sentence of its own. V3b's invariant held only if a mark is cleared nowhere but in the recomputation of a range; the design wrote the clearing as a step of that recomputation and did not say that nothing else clears it, and the review asked for the sentence, so that a later path (a removal) does not clear it (PR #373).

Before a design PR states an invariant, each path that could break it is followed in the design's own terms, nested and unused cases included. V3b's first sentence said a marked node stays under an unmarked one only when the unmarked one is a scroll container; following a nested scroll container whose range nothing reads showed that the marked nodes are that container and its content, and the sentence was corrected before the PR (PR #373).

A sentence added to the design to answer a review is a claim like any other and is checked before it goes in. The L5 re-review explained that a leaf an ancestor lays out first has "nothing to compare", and the design took that over; the parent's measurement cache still held a value of the old content, and the random test with several changes per pass showed the wrong rectangle (PR #109, #115).

After a script edits a Markdown table, read `git diff --stat`. A diff of the whole table means its columns were padded again (it happened in S9 to `docs/roadmap.md`, by no step of the script): restore the file and replace only the rows that change.

A design PR writes what the Grill decided. A better shape found while writing does not replace a decision in the PR, even with a note in its body: it goes to the owner as a question with a recommendation (`.cursor/rules/consent.mdc`). V1c's design moved a text leaf's own color from `TextLeaf.Color` to `NodeVisual.Foreground`, against the Grill's Q14, and said so only in the PR body; the review found that each text leaf with a color would then hold a `NodeVisual`, and the owner kept Q14 (PR #319). Before the design PR is opened, each answer of the Grill is read against the sentence of the design that writes it, one by one, and a difference goes to the owner. K1's design sent FocusIn and FocusOut in capture and bubble against Q10's bubble, and its body did not say so; the review found it (PR #352).

A design that makes a value of an existing type from another source (a Click from a key) gives every field of the type a value. K1's first design gave the keyboard Click its `ClickCount`, `Position`, and `Button`, and left `PointerType`, `Buttons`, and `Modifiers` open (PR #352's review).

A design that adds a value to a type that several events share says, for each event, whether it sets the value and where it reads it. K1 added `KeyModifiers.Meta` for the keys and did not say whether pointer events, which share `KeyModifiers`, read the Windows keys too (PR #352's review). A condition over a node's ancestors says whether it covers the node itself: K1's "has no `Hidden` ancestor" left a `Hidden` node focusable, against §7.1's "skips `Hidden` subtrees" (PR #352's review).

What the framework does to an input before the handlers receive it (moving the focus on a press) says how an app stops it, or that it cannot. K1's first design moved the focus before the route and did not say that `.Focusable(false)` is the only way out; zed's button stops it with `prevent_default` (PR #352's review).

A row's design says where its input is injected in tests (design §12.4) and which test layer of §12.1 checks each part. K1's Grill asked nothing about §12 until the owner pointed it out.

## Citing other frameworks

A citation that a delegate's research gives is read again at the tag before it is written: the path, the line, and that the line says what the sentence claims. A library the design cites at more than one version (Taffy 0.9.0, 0.13.0, and 0.15.1) is cited with its version. In D3 the review found a Taffy line of 0.13.0 written with no version, an Avalonia line that pointed at another method, and lines of `a11y.rs` and `text_system.rs` that did not hold the claim. One of the review's own corrections named the wrong file, and reading the source again found the right one (PR #442). The citation is written with the version, the path, and the line, a delegate's research included; a name alone (`advance_clock_to_next_timer`, `debug_selector`) leaves the next reader to search for it (PR #457).

## Judge the current optimum

The design records a decision made at that time. It is not proof that the decision is still the best one.

- Recommend the best solution for the problem as it stands now.
- Do not cite an existing sentence as the reason to keep that shape.
- Do not add public API only so an older shape can stay.
- If the written decision is no longer the best one, say so and change the design through the process above.
